<item>
  <title><![CDATA[The Weight of Wool]]></title>
  <link>http://localhost:8885/2026/10/29/the-weight-of-wool/</link>
  <pubDate>Thu, 29 Oct 2026 09:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
  <dc:creator>marketing</dc:creator>
  <guid isPermaLink="false">http://localhost:8885/2026/10/29/the-weight-of-wool/</guid>
  <description/>
  <content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>There is a moment every October when the first heavy coat comes out of storage and the body remembers what winter feels like. Wool has a gravity to it — not just physical weight, but a kind of moral seriousness.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>A good melton coat is built to outlast trends. Its fibers are felted, dense, almost architectural. It holds its shape on the hanger the way a building holds its line against the sky.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:pullquote -->
<figure class="wp-block-pullquote"><blockquote><p>Buy less. Choose the heavier cloth. Wear it for ten years.</p></blockquote></figure>
<!-- /wp:pullquote -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>This season we are drawn to wool in its quieter registers: oatmeal, slate, a blue so deep it reads as black under streetlights.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->]]></content:encoded>
