<item>
  <title><![CDATA[Color as Sediment]]></title>
  <link>http://localhost:8885/2026/10/08/color-as-sediment/</link>
  <pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 09:15:00 +0000</pubDate>
  <dc:creator>marketing</dc:creator>
  <guid isPermaLink="false">http://localhost:8885/2026/10/08/color-as-sediment/</guid>
  <description/>
  <content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Stack your knitwear and look at it sideways. The colors settle into strata: rust over moss over sand. A wardrobe built slowly ends up looking like geology.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>We love palettes that seem found rather than chosen — the faded terracotta of an old pot, the green of lichen on a north-facing wall. These are colors that age well because they already look aged.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:quote -->
<blockquote class="wp-block-quote"><!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The best palettes are borrowed from things that have been outside for a long time.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></blockquote>
<!-- /wp:quote -->]]></content:encoded>
