<item>
  <title><![CDATA[Stripes, Reconsidered]]></title>
  <link>http://localhost:8885/2026/10/01/stripes-reconsidered/</link>
  <pubDate>Thu, 01 Oct 2026 10:30:00 +0000</pubDate>
  <dc:creator>marketing</dc:creator>
  <guid isPermaLink="false">http://localhost:8885/2026/10/01/stripes-reconsidered/</guid>
  <description/>
  <content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The Breton stripe has been so thoroughly co-opted that it is easy to forget it was once workwear: practical, legible at sea, cheap to make.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Up close, a woven stripe is something else entirely. It is a record of the loom — warp and weft agreeing, for a few rows, to change color together.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>We are drawn to stripes that reveal their making: slubbed, irregular, slightly off. Perfection is for print. Weave should show its hand.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->]]></content:encoded>
